# CLAUDE.md

# MANDATORY behavioral rules
Apply to every task unless explicitly overridden. Bias: caution over speed on non-trivial work; judgment on trivial tasks. Rule numbers are referenced from project docs — never renumber.
## Rule 1 — Think Before Coding
State assumptions explicitly. If uncertain, ask rather than guess. Present multiple interpretations when ambiguity exists. Push back when a simpler approach exists. Stop when confused. Name what's unclear.
## Rule 2 — Simplicity First
Minimum code that solves the problem. Nothing speculative. No features beyond what was asked. No abstractions for single-use code. Test: would a senior engineer say this is overcomplicated? If yes, simplify.
## Rule 3 — Surgical Changes
Touch only what you must. Clean up only your own mess. Don't "improve" adjacent code, comments, or formatting. Don't refactor what isn't broken. Match existing style.
## Rule 4 — Goal-Driven Execution
Define success criteria, then loop until verified. (About HOW to reach a goal; does not override the ordered procedures below, e.g. Rule 16 or subagent-driven execution.)
## Rule 5 — Model only for judgment
Use the model for classification, drafting, summarization, extraction. If code can answer (routing, retries, deterministic transforms), code answers.
## Rule 6 — (retired; number reserved)
## Rule 7 — Surface conflicts, don't average them
When two patterns contradict, pick one (more recent / more tested), say why, flag the other for cleanup. Never blend.
## Rule 8 — Read before you write
Read exports, immediate callers, shared utilities first. "Looks orthogonal" is dangerous. If unsure why code is structured a way, ask.
## Rule 9 — Tests verify intent
Tests encode WHY behavior matters. A test that cannot fail when the business logic changes is wrong.
## Rule 10 — Checkpoint after every significant step
State what's done, verified, left. Don't continue from a state you can't describe back.
## Rule 11 — Match the codebase's conventions
Conformance > taste. Surface conventions you think harmful; don't fork silently.
## Rule 12 — Fail loud
Never report "completed"/"tests pass" if anything was skipped. Surface uncertainty.
## Rule 13 — No dismissed or unmaintained dependencies
Before adopting a library/framework/plugin/tool, verify upstream activity (recent releases and commits, issue triage, deprecation notices). If archived, slow-maintenance, or superseded, say so before proposing it and pick the maintained successor. Flag existing ones for replacement instead of working around their bugs.
## Rule 14 — Avoid GPL-licensed code
No GPL-family dependencies, snippets, or assets (GPLv2/v3, AGPL, statically linked LGPL, any propagating copyleft). Verify every third-party component is permissive (Apache 2.0, MIT, BSD, MPL 2.0 file-scope, EPL 2.0, …); if not, surface it and propose a permissive alternative. When in doubt, ask.
## Rule 15 — Implementer subagents use MCP tools
Every implementer prompt MUST include:
> Before reading or editing any project source file, use Serena's symbolic tools (`get_symbols_overview` → `find_symbol` with `include_body=true` → `replace_symbol_body` / `insert_*`). Built-in `Read` / `Grep` / `Edit` are allowed only for non-code files or after Serena failed on that target. Before writing code that calls into `<library X>`, query `context7` for the API of the version pinned in the project's build manifest. If the plan's code conflicts with `context7` or the real source, follow Rule 7: use the real API and report the deviation.

Not required for: pure refactoring that doesn't change library calls, new scripts, business-logic debugging, code review.
## Rule 16 — Contain CI cost: defer push, batch commits
Every push to a PR branch or a CI-triggering branch fires the full workflow matrix. Applies to implementer subagents too (they commit locally and report; they never push).
1. Open the PR last: accumulate task commits locally, verify each with the local test suite (e.g. `mvn -pl <module> -am -DskipITs verify`); open the PR when implementation is complete or when a CI-only check (e.g. Testcontainers ITs this box can't run) is the blocker.
2. Batch pushes (2–3 commits per push) and batch CI fixups into one push.
3. Before pushing, squash WIP commits ("typo", "fix compile") into their task commit; keep one task = one commit.
4. When pushing, state what is pushed and why.
5. Exceptions: branch protection requiring a green run; user asks to push; direct-to-main files (see Git rules).

# Sprints, issues, tests
- Work is organized in sprints; each plan Task is a GitHub Issue that Claude creates (`gh issue create`, before execution) — never ask the user to open them. Body: objective + `- [ ]` checklist.
- Tick each checklist item (`gh issue edit`) as soon as it's implemented and committed. All items MUST be `- [x]` before opening a PR or declaring a task done.
- Every feature/bugfix ships with tests. Plain DTOs or integration-only code (live-DB adapters, headless-untestable UI) may rely on covering tests — say so in the plan and make sure they exist.

# Git and PR rules
- Every code change on a dedicated branch, merged to `main` via PR. Exception: `README.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `docs/HANDOFF*.md` go directly to `main` (no branch, PR, or issue).
- No co-author references and no "Generated with Claude Code" footer in commits or PRs.
- Group correlated issues (same theme AND overlapping files) into one PR by default; propose the grouping and confirm first. One task = one commit inside it. PR body has `Closes #N` for every grouped issue.
- Technical/operational docs for a feature go on that feature's branch/PR (not direct-to-main, not a separate PR while it's open); only after merge does a follow-up PR carry them.
- Claude creates PRs; the user merges — NEVER merge autonomously. Only after the user confirms the merge: delete the branch locally and on the remote, checkout `main`, pull.

# Tools
- Always use context7 for library/framework docs, and Serena's symbolic tools for code in the active project (`Read` only for small/non-code files).
- To explore another codebase (e.g. Apache Hop) use Serena `query_project` — see the `serena-query-project` skill. Never `Read`/`Grep` the foreign codebase.
- Bash: never chain independent commands with `&&`/`;`; use parallel calls. Chain only on a real exit-code dependency.

# Workflow skills
- Brainstorming → `superpowers:brainstorming`. Plans → `superpowers:writing-plans`; commit plans and specs to git.
- Plan execution is ALWAYS subagent-driven (`superpowers:subagent-driven-development`), one fresh subagent per Task, review between tasks. Never ask which execution mode — this overrides any skill prompt offering a choice.

# Model routing — MANDATORY
- The main agent (Opus) acts EXCLUSIVELY as architect, judge, and orchestrator: design, planning, decomposition, reviewing and accepting subagent output, talking to the user.
- Delegate hands-on work to Sonnet subagents (Agent tool, `model: "sonnet"`): implementation, tests, exploration/searches, reading/summarizing documents, debugging legwork. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL=sonnet` is the fallback for skill-launched subagents.
- The main agent writes no production or test code inline; it may run a few targeted reads/commands only to verify a subagent's result.

# Documentation
- Agent-consumed docs (CLAUDE.md, README, CHANGELOG, ADRs, plans, handoffs, runbooks): Markdown, Mermaid diagrams. Human-consumed deliverables (stakeholder/customer docs, reports, guides): HTML with SVG diagrams. Mixed audience → Markdown, HTML export only on request.
- GUI mockups: Draw.io (`.drawio`) files.

# Language and tone
- The live conversation with the user is in Italian. EVERYTHING else is English — code, comments, commits, branches, PR/issue titles, bodies and comments, all documentation (including human deliverables). Switch to English before writing any file, commit, PR, or issue, even mid-session.
- In conversation, be explanatory and colloquial rather than terse: explain the why, use plain language and concrete examples, like talking to a colleague. Artifacts keep their normal concise technical register.
